# Cloudflare Workers static-assets caching and security headers.
# Cloudflare applies every matching block, so the site-wide security headers in
# the /* rule below combine with the per-path cache rules that follow.

# Security headers for every response. Deliberately not a Content-Security-
# Policy: the game loads Google's AdSense/H5 Games Ads tag at runtime, which
# pulls in further Google origins and frames, and a strict CSP breaks rewarded
# video in ways that are painful to keep working. These four headers harden the
# site without touching what the ad tag needs.
#   nosniff        — a response is used as its declared type, never sniffed into
#                    an executable one.
#   frame-ancestors — SAMEORIGIN stops another site from embedding the game in
#                    an iframe (clickjacking, and hotlinking the whole app).
#   referrer-policy — never leak the full URL to third parties.
#   permissions    — switch off powerful device APIs the game never uses; the
#                    ad-relevant features (e.g. Topics) are left untouched.
#   hsts           — once a browser has seen this header it refuses to talk to
#                    the domain over plain HTTP for a year, which closes the
#                    one request an attacker on the network can still touch:
#                    the very first one, before the redirect to HTTPS. The CSP
#                    reasoning above does not apply to it — it loads nothing
#                    and constrains nothing the ad tag needs.
#                    `includeSubDomains` is safe here because Cloudflare
#                    terminates TLS for every host on the zone, `www` included
#                    when it is added. `preload` is deliberately absent: it is
#                    a submission to a list baked into browser binaries and is
#                    slow and painful to reverse, which is the wrong trade for
#                    a one-person project that may yet need a plain-HTTP host.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# Hashed build assets never change; index.html must always revalidate so a
# deploy is picked up immediately. Crests and fonts are stable files.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/crests/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
# The service worker must always revalidate, for the same reason index.html
# does and more urgently: a cached worker keeps serving the cache rules of the
# build that shipped it, so a stale one can outlive the deploy that fixed it.
# Browsers bypass the HTTP cache for the worker script on most update checks
# anyway; this makes it true on all of them.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
